# ============================================================================
# LibreRun — observability vendor settings, THE TRACE LEG (blueprint S7a, L26)
#
#   cp observability-traces.env.example observability-traces.env
#
# Loaded by the `otel-bridge` service and nothing else. Its companion,
# `observability.env`, holds the log leg's settings and is loaded by
# `vector` alone.
#
# TWO FILES, NOT ONE, and that is the point rather than an
# inconvenience: L28 says no process receives a secret it does not read,
# and Gate P (`backend/tests/test_secret_partition.py`) enforces it. One
# shared file would put the Splunk Observability access token in
# Vector's environment and the HEC token in the bridge's, where neither
# is ever read. Each file goes to the one process that reads it.
#
#   observability.env          -> vector       logs
#   observability-traces.env   -> otel-bridge  traces
#
# Both are optional (`env_file: required: false`).
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CLASSES (K4, decision L29)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every variable in the deployment sits in exactly one class and in
# exactly one example file; `.env.example`'s header explains the four.
# This file carries two of them, side by side per vendor:
#
#   Class 1, BOOTSTRAP — where to send it. The collector resolves these
#     into its exporter configuration as it starts.
#   Class 4, SECRETS — what authenticates the send. Read by
#     `otel-bridge` and handed to no other process.
#
# (Written "Class N" here because the banner lines below are the
# machine-readable headers, and a second copy of one in a paragraph
# would open a block of nothing.)
#
# The SELECTOR is not here and not in observability.env either: set
# LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR to datadog, elastic or splunk in `.env`, because
# Compose expands it while building the bridge's command line, before
# this container exists. See observability.env.example's header.
#
# WHAT THE BRIDGE IS: a stock OpenTelemetry Collector, the one component
# in the box that emits spec-compliant OTLP protobuf. Vector wire-shapes
# OTLP/JSON to it and it re-exports protobuf with the vendor's own auth
# header, because Splunk Observability's /v2/trace/otlp documents
# protobuf and Vector has no OTLP protobuf encoder.
#
# WHAT THE MOCKS IN CI PROVE: that the wire shape LibreRun emits is the
# one each vendor documents. They are not a vendor's acceptance of your
# data — only your own account can tell you that.
# ============================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datadog  (LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR=datadog)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datadog's trace intake is not an OTLP endpoint: the documented OTLP
# path is a Datadog Agent's own receiver. The Agent holds DD_API_KEY and
# DD_SITE — set those where the Agent runs, not here — so the bridge
# sends it no credential and needs none. That is why this section has a
# URL and nothing else, and no class-[4] block at all.
#
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# The Agent's OTLP/HTTP traces URL, full path. The Agent needs its OTLP
# receiver switched on: `otlp_config.receiver.protocols.http` in
# datadog.yaml, or the Agent's own
# DD_OTLP_CONFIG_RECEIVER_PROTOCOLS_HTTP_ENDPOINT environment variable
# set to 0.0.0.0:4318 — written as prose because it is the AGENT's
# variable and this file delivers only the bridge's.
# DD_AGENT_OTLP_URL=http://datadog-agent:4318/v1/traces

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Elastic  (LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR=elastic)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# The APM OTLP intake URL, full path — /v1/traces on an APM server, or
# the Elastic Cloud managed OTLP endpoint's traces path.
# ELASTIC_APM_OTLP_URL=https://my-deployment.apm.us-east-1.aws.found.io/v1/traces
# `ApiKey` is Elastic's documented scheme. A self-managed APM server
# taking a secret token sets Bearer here and puts the token in
# ELASTIC_APM_API_KEY.
# ELASTIC_APM_AUTH_SCHEME=ApiKey
#
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# The APM key is its OWN variable rather than the cluster's
# ELASTIC_API_KEY: Elastic issues them separately, and naming them
# separately is what lets each process be handed only the one it reads.
# ELASTIC_APM_API_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splunk  (LIBRERUN_OBS_VENDOR=splunk)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# Splunk Observability's OTLP trace ingest, full path.
# SPLUNK_OTLP_URL=https://ingest.us1.observability.splunkcloud.com/v2/trace/otlp
#
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# The access token rides the X-SF-Token header Splunk documents — a
# DIFFERENT credential from the HEC token the log leg uses, which is why
# it is in this file and the HEC token is in the other one.
# SPLUNK_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cribl  (VECTOR_CRIBL=1 in `.env`, profile `cribl`)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The trace half of the built-in Cribl overlay: every span goes to a
# Cribl OpenTelemetry source configured with Protocol: gRPC (its
# default; any OTLP version). The log half — the HEC endpoint and token
# — is in observability.env, because `vector` reads that one and this
# process never does. Moved out of `.env` at K4 for the same reason.
#
# [1] BOOTSTRAP — must be in the environment
# gRPC target host:port, NO path — e.g.
# default.main.<org>.cribl.cloud:4317. TLS is the default; an https://
# prefix also works.
# CRIBL_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
#
# [4] SECRETS — where each one lives
# Optional source auth token, sent as "Authorization: Bearer <token>".
# Ignored by sources with auth off.
# CRIBL_OTLP_TOKEN=
